nerated as the final residence of Mary, the mother of Jesus, and has been a focal point of Christian devotion for centuries. Its discovery in the 19th century rekindled interest in the historical context of Mary and the early Christian community, thereby illuminating aspects of religious practice during the formative years of Christianity.

